join:round"/></svg>', 'Graph of relative intensity against wavelength for an X-ray tube: a continuous spectrum from a cut-off wavelength with two sharp characteristic lines.'),
('7cdd54b6-2ffc-51ec-a739-60e14c6593ff', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 30, 30, 'An X-ray tube emits 12 W of X-rays with a mean photon energy of 40 keV. How many photons does it emit per second? Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.875e15', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 40 × 10³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 6.4 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. n = 12 ÷ 6.4 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 1.88 × 10¹⁵. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: keV to J. C1: n = P/E. A1: n = 1.88 × 10¹⁵.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('382eef40-2451-590b-bc8e-789cd779f3c8', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 31, 31, 'An X-ray exposure at 50 kV and 100 mA lasts 0.50 s. 99% of the energy becomes heat in an anode of heat capacity 300 J K⁻¹. Calculate the temperature rise of the anode.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '8.25', 'K', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Energy = 0.99 × 50 × 10³ × 0.100 × 0.50 = 2475 J. ΔT = 2475 ÷ 300 = 8.25 K. Mark scheme (4 marks). C1: energy = VIt. C1: 99% to heat. C1: ΔT = E/C. A1: ΔT = 8.25 K.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('81c8fd39-47db-5aec-b338-3875deedb04e', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 32, 32, 'Why does the anode of a powerful X-ray tube rotate?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'To spread the heating over a larger area of the target', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'The focal spot moves around the rim, so no single point overheats. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: heat spread. C1: target not damaged. A1: D (To spread the heating over a larger area of the target).', 3, '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 440 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="fg9fb20a72-t fg9fb20a72-d" style="font-family:ui-sans-serif,system-ui,-apple-system,''Segoe UI'',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;max-width:100%;height:auto"><title id="fg9fb20a72-t">X-ray tube</title><desc id="fg9fb20a72-d">An X-ray tube: the electron beam strikes a small focal spot on the tungsten target of the anode, where almost all of its energy becomes heat.</desc><rect x="40" y="50" width="330" height="100" rx="40" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:1.4;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="56" y="62" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-muted,#6b7280)">vacuum</text><path d="M80,90 q5,-10 10,0 t10,0 t10,0" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="80" y1="90" x2="60" y2="90" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="110" y1="90" x2="120" y2="80" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="95" y="122" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">cathode</text><polygon points="290,70 330,70 330,130 290,110" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.0;fill:var(--fig-fill,rgba(29,78,216,0.10));stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><line x1="330" y1="100" x2="385" y2="100" style="stroke:currentColor;stroke-width:2.6;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="318" y="58" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:currentColor">anode (tungsten target)</text><line x1="115" y1="96.4" x2="279.8" y2="89" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="284,88.8 278.7,91.6 278.5,86.5" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="115" y1="100" x2="279.8" y2="96.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="284,96 278.6,98.7 278.5,93.6" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><line x1="115" y1="103.6" x2="279.8" y2="103.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="284,103.2 278.6,105.7 278.6,100.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8);stroke:none"/><text x="200" y="120" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent,#1d4ed8)">electrons</text><line x1="282" y1="106" x2="264.5" y2="203.2" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="263.6,208 261.8,201.2 267.6,202.3" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="296" y1="106" x2="286.5" y2="203.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="286,208 283.7,201.4 289.6,202" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="310" y1="106" x2="308.5" y2="203.1" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:0.9;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><polygon points="308.4,208 305.5,201.6 311.5,201.7" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke:none"/><line x1="262" y1="150" x2="318" y2="150" style="stroke:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309);stroke-width:2.0;fill:none;stroke-linecap:round;stroke-linejoin:round"/><text x="345" y="210" font-size="12" text-anchor="start" dominant-baseline="central" style="fill:var(--fig-accent-2,#b45309)">X-rays</text></svg>', 'An X-ray tube: the electron beam strikes a small focal spot on the tungsten target of the anode, where almost all of its energy becomes heat.'),
('99d52876-23e8-5377-88c5-44dd69373736', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 33, 33, 'In a low-voltage demonstration X-ray tube, electrons are accelerated from rest through 5.0 kV. Calculate their speed at the target, in units of 10⁷ m s⁻¹ (ignore relativity). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and mₑ = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '4.191', '10⁷ m s⁻¹', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'v = √(2eV/m) = √(2 × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 5.0 × 10³ ÷ 9.11 × 10⁻³¹) = 4.19 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: eV = ½mv². C1: substitution. A1: v = 4.19 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('39f774d2-6216-50a6-baf2-d48c245dd080', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 34, 34, 'A characteristic line of tungsten has a photon energy of 59 keV. Calculate its wavelength in units of 10⁻¹¹ m. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.107', '10⁻¹¹ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λ = hc/E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (59 × 10³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 2.11 × 10⁻¹¹ m. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: keV to J. C1: λ = hc/E. A1: λ = 2.11 × 10⁻¹¹ m.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('78373971-a5bb-5d05-a551-708c5517b030', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 35, 35, 'A patient drinks a barium compound before an X-ray of the stomach. Why does this help?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'Barium has a high proton number and absorbs X-rays strongly, outlining the stomach', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Attenuation rises steeply with proton number. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: high Z. C1: strong absorption gives contrast. A1: B (Barium has a high proton number and absorbs X-rays strongly, outlining the stomach).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ea23b0b3-3c10-5dd2-8c3d-379ee460cc09', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 36, 36, 'An exposure uses a tube current of 200 mA for 0.050 s. Calculate the charge that flows through the tube, in mC.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '10 mC', 'mC', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Q = It = 200 × 10⁻³ × 0.050 = 0.010 C. In mC: 0.010 × 10³ = 10. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: Q = It. C1: C to mC. A1: Q = 10 mC.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('49b813bd-8742-5f1f-aa17-921b3a4a1b17', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 37, 37, 'In an exposure, 0.010 C of electrons is accelerated through 90 kV. Calculate the energy delivered to the target.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '900', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = QV = 0.010 × 90 × 10³ = 900 J. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = QV. C1: kV to V. A1: E = 900 J.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('29011314-2c26-5526-a48e-05b0e335d3c0', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 38, 38, 'Why is an X-ray tube surrounded by lead shielding with a small window?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'So that X-rays leave only in the useful direction, protecting staff and the patient', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'Lead strongly absorbs X-rays. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: absorbs stray X-rays. C1: beam only through window. A1: C (So that X-rays leave only in the useful direction, protecting staff and the patient).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('e8f4016a-a2de-5477-b4ff-592020f4a7c2', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 39, 39, 'Calculate (cut-off wavelength at 50 kV) ÷ (cut-off wavelength at 100 kV).', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'λ(min) = hc/eV ∝ 1/V: ratio = 100 ÷ 50 = 2.0. Mark scheme (4 marks). M1: λ(min) ∝ 1/V. C1: ratio of voltages. C1: value. A1: ratio = 2.0.', 4, NULL, NULL),
('1c75bc01-4834-5ce6-94a1-3af30e0bb572', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 40, 40, 'What is the main advantage of a CT scan over a single X-ray photograph?', NULL, 2, 'multiple_choice'::answer_type, 'It shows separate slices, so overlapping organs do not hide each other', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, 'A radiograph is a single shadow of everything along each path. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: slices / 3-D information. C1: no overlap of structures. A1: A (It shows separate slices, so overlapping organs do not hide each other).', 3, NULL, NULL),
('ce82ce44-74b2-54cc-a78f-683cb0278a3b', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 41, 41, 'An X-ray photon has a wavelength of 0.060 nm. Calculate its energy in keV. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 2, 'numeric'::answer_type, '20.72', 'keV', false, NULL, false, NULL, 'E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ 0.060 × 10⁻⁹ = 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. In keV: 3.315 × 10⁻¹⁵ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁶ = 20.7. Mark scheme (3 marks). C1: E = hc/λ. C1: J to keV. A1: E = 20.7 keV.', 3, NULL, NULL),
('199a516f-64ec-51e9-83d1-a0d72027d220', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 42, 42, 'An X-ray tube operates at 80 kV with a current of 25 mA and an efficiency of 1.0%. (a) Calculate the input power. (b) Calculate the X-ray power. (c) Calculate the heat produced in the anode during a 5.0 s exposure.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9900', 'J', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = 80 × 10³ × 25 × 10⁻³ = 2000 W. (b) 0.010 × 2000 = 20 W. (c) Heat = (2000 − 20) × 5.0 = 9900 J. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: input power. C1: X-ray power. M1: heat rate = input − X-ray. C1: value. A1: heat = 9900 J.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('30d6cd8d-320a-5865-8bbb-00bada76f2b6', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 43, 43, 'A tube at 100 kV produces 5.0 W of X-rays. Assume the mean photon energy is one third of the maximum. (a) Calculate the maximum photon energy in J. (b) Calculate the mean photon energy. (c) Calculate the number of photons emitted per second. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '9.375e14', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 100 × 10³ = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁴ J. (b) 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 3 = 5.333 × 10⁻¹⁵ J. (c) n = 5.0 ÷ 5.333 × 10⁻¹⁵ = 9.37 × 10¹⁴. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: E(max) = eV. C1: mean energy. M1: n = P/E. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: n = 9.38 × 10¹⁴.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('b8c5144e-b58f-5f36-a03c-6ee73068a409', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 44, 44, 'A radiograph uses a tube current of 300 mA for 0.10 s. (a) Calculate the charge passing through the tube. (b) Calculate the number of electrons that strike the target. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.875e17', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Q = 300 × 10⁻³ × 0.10 = 0.030 C. (b) N = 0.030 ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 1.88 × 10¹⁷. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: Q = It. C1: value. M1: N = Q/e. C1: value. A1: N = 1.88 × 10¹⁷.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('56f82dd3-1f16-51a0-9802-e3c3d8fb8d26', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 45, 45, 'In tungsten the K shell is at −69.5 keV and the L shell at −11.5 keV. (a) Calculate the energy of the photon emitted when an electron drops from L to K. (b) Calculate its wavelength in units of 10⁻¹¹ m. Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.143', '10⁻¹¹ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 69.5 − 11.5 = 58.0 keV. (b) λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (58.0 × 10³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 2.14 × 10⁻¹¹ m. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: energy difference. C1: keV to J. M1: λ = hc/E. C1: value. A1: λ = 2.14 × 10⁻¹¹ m.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('84461b22-03ae-5779-a974-f0db6e9340dc', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 46, 46, 'For the tungsten K line to appear, an incoming electron must be able to remove a K-shell electron (binding energy 69.5 keV). (a) Explain why a tube at 60 kV shows no K lines. (b) State the minimum tube voltage for K lines, in kV. (c) Calculate the cut-off wavelength at that voltage, in units of 10⁻¹¹ m. Give only the answer to (c). Take h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.789', '10⁻¹¹ m', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) Electrons of 60 keV cannot ionise the K shell. (b) 69.5 kV. (c) λ = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁸ ÷ (1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 69.5 × 10³) = 1.79 × 10⁻¹¹ m. Mark scheme (6 marks). B1: electron energy < K binding energy. C1: 69.5 kV. M1: λ = hc/eV. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: λ(min) = 1.79 × 10⁻¹¹ m.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('c5bce526-2445-5443-853d-e5f5a28bee8f', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 47, 47, 'A student measures the cut-off wavelength of an X-ray tube at 40 kV as 0.0310 nm. (a) Write the equation linking the cut-off wavelength and the voltage. (b) Use the measurement to find the Planck constant, in units of 10⁻³⁴ J s. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '6.613', '10⁻³⁴ J s', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) eV = hc/λ(min). (b) h = eVλ/c = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 40 × 10³ × 0.0310 × 10⁻⁹ ÷ 3.00 × 10⁸ = 6.61 × 10⁻³⁴ J s. Mark scheme (5 marks). B1: equation. M1: rearranged for h. C1: substitution. C1: value. A1: h = 6.61 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('c22f6fe5-3ad7-50ca-bf9b-2532a0fac50c', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 48, 48, 'An X-ray tube has a 1.0% efficiency and must give an X-ray power of 15 W at 75 kV. (a) Calculate the input power needed. (b) Calculate the tube current in mA. (c) Calculate the rate at which heat is produced.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '20 mA', 'mA', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) P = 15 ÷ 0.010 = 1500 W. (b) I = 1500 ÷ 75 × 10³ = 0.020 A. In mA: 0.020 × 10³ = 20. (c) 1500 − 15 = 1485 W. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: input power. M1: I = P/V. C1: A to mA. C1: value. C1: heat rate. A1: I = 20 mA.', 6, NULL, NULL),
('cc483722-0716-57af-9c8d-8468c112e6fc', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 49, 49, 'A beam has photons of maximum energy 120 keV. (a) State the tube voltage. (b) Calculate the cut-off frequency in units of 10¹⁹ Hz. (c) Calculate the cut-off wavelength. Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C and h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '2.896', '10¹⁹ Hz', false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) 120 kV. (b) f = eV/h = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 120 × 10³ ÷ 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 2.90 × 10¹⁹ Hz. (c) λ = c/f ≈ 1.04 × 10⁻¹¹ m. Mark scheme (5 marks). C1: voltage. M1: f = eV/h. C1: value. C1: λ = c/f. A1: f(max) = 2.90 × 10¹⁹ Hz.', 5, NULL, NULL),
('18764aff-a719-520a-9e08-caf3078ecad6', '1fd2e146-9233-4bc7-8fd2-b600f61a7d73', 'fed684c4-735b-500a-85f8-bf99a91029e7', 'a-level', '30.1', '9702 coursebook 30.1 · X-ray imaging and CT', 50, 50, 'An X-ray tube is used at 60 kV and 50 mA for 0.20 s. (a) Calculate the energy supplied. (b) Calculate the energy of the X-rays if 0.80% is converted. (c) Taking a mean photon energy of 25 keV, calculate the number of X-ray photons produced. Give only the answer to (c). Take e = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.', NULL, 3, 'numeric'::answer_type, '1.2e15', NULL, false, NULL, false, NULL, '(a) E = 60 × 10³ × 50 × 10⁻³ × 0.20 = 600 J. (b) 0.0080 × 600 = 4.8 J. (c) N = 4.8 ÷ (25 × 10³ × 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹) = 1.20 × 10¹⁵. Mark scheme (6 marks). C1: E = VIt. C1: X-ray energy. C1: photon energy in J. M1: N = E/E(photon). C1: value. A1: N = 1.20 × 10¹⁵.', 6, NULL, NULL)
